steps.body:
RAII, from *Constructors, Destructors, and RAII*, is the pattern most of C++ rests on: a constructor acquires something and the destructor gives it back, so the release runs on every way out of a scope, a `return`, a `break`, or an exception. `std::unique_ptr` applies it to heap memory. The same shape fits anything that must be undone: a flag that pauses trading while a config reloads, a stream's fill character, a count of open requests.

A **scope guard** is RAII for a one-off cleanup. Its constructor stores a lambda, and its destructor calls it:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

int main() {
    bool tradingOn = true;
    {
        tradingOn = false;
        ScopeGuard resume([&] { tradingOn = true; });
        std::cout << std::boolalpha << "inside " << tradingOn << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "after " << tradingOn << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename F>
class ScopeGuard {
public:
    explicit ScopeGuard(F cleanup) : cleanup_(cleanup) {}
    ~ScopeGuard() { cleanup_(); }
    ScopeGuard(const ScopeGuard&) = delete;
    ScopeGuard& operator=(const ScopeGuard&) = delete;

private:
    F cleanup_;
};
```

Writing `ScopeGuard resume([&] { tradingOn = true; });` lets class template argument deduction fill in `F` from the lambda's type. The deleted copies matter, because a copied guard would run the cleanup twice.

- The cleanup runs inside a destructor, so it must not throw, for the reason *Exceptions and Error Handling* gave.
- Give the guard a name. `ScopeGuard([&] { ... });` without one is a temporary, and it runs the cleanup at the end of that same statement.
- When the same cleanup appears in many places, write a class with a name instead, such as a `FillGuard` that restores `std::cout.fill()`.

Worth it whenever a change must be undone on every path out of a function. *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes*, in the Concurrency section, uses `std::lock_guard`, the same pattern for a mutex.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

template <typename F>
class ScopeGuard {
public:
    explicit ScopeGuard(F cleanup) : cleanup_(cleanup) {}
    ~ScopeGuard() { cleanup_(); }
    ScopeGuard(const ScopeGuard&) = delete;
    ScopeGuard& operator=(const ScopeGuard&) = delete;

private:
    F cleanup_;
};

struct Engine {
    bool tradingOn = true;
    int version = 1;
};

void reload(Engine& engine, int version) {
    engine.tradingOn = false;
    ScopeGuard resume([&engine] {
        engine.tradingOn = true;
        std::cout << "  trading resumed\n";
    });
    std::cout << "reloading v" << version << '\n';
    if (version == engine.version) {
        std::cout << "  already loaded\n";
        return;
    }
    if (version < 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("bad version");
    }
    engine.version = version;
    std::cout << "  loaded v" << version << '\n';
}

int main() {
    Engine engine;
    reload(engine, 2);
    reload(engine, 2);
    try {
        reload(engine, -1);
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "  error: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << "trading on: " << engine.tradingOn << '\n';
    return 0;
}

