steps.body:
An execution engine sizes every order, and the desk wants to change the rule without touching the engine: a fixed quantity today, a fixed amount of cash tomorrow. That is the **strategy** pattern. The algorithm sits behind a fixed interface, so the code that uses it never changes when you swap it. C++ offers two forms.

- **At run time**, through an abstract base `SizingRule` with a pure virtual `int size(double price) const` and one derived class per rule, held in a `std::unique_ptr<SizingRule>`. The rule can come from a config file, and every call is a virtual call.
- **At compile time**, through a template, `template <typename Rule> int sizeOrder(const Rule& rule, double price)`. Any class with a matching `size` works, with no base class. The compiler stamps out a copy per rule and can inline the call, but the rule is fixed when you build.

A **factory** goes with the run-time form. It is a function that turns data, such as a name read from a config file, into the right derived class, so the rest of the program only ever sees the base:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class SizingRule {
public:
    virtual ~SizingRule() = default;
    virtual int size(double price) const = 0;
};

class FixedQty : public SizingRule {
public:
    explicit FixedQty(int qty) : qty_(qty) {}
    int size(double) const override { return qty_; }

private:
    int qty_;
};

class FixedCash : public SizingRule {
public:
    explicit FixedCash(double cash) : cash_(cash) {}
    int size(double price) const override { return static_cast<int>(cash_ / price); }

private:
    double cash_;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    const auto rule = makeRule("cash", 5000);
    std::cout << rule->size(125.0) << " shares\n";
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << (makeRule("kelly", 0.5) == nullptr) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<SizingRule> makeRule(const std::string& name, double param) {
    if (name == "fixed") {
        return std::make_unique<FixedQty>(static_cast<int>(param));
    }
    if (name == "cash") {
        return std::make_unique<FixedCash>(param);
    }
    return nullptr;  // unknown: the caller decides what to do
}
```

Adding a rule takes one new class and one more `if` in the factory, and nothing else changes. An engine that holds its rule as a member is **composition**, the reuse *Multiple Inheritance, the Diamond, and Virtual Bases* ended on: the engine has a sizing rule instead of being one, so a new rule never needs a new engine class. Choose the template when the rule is known at build time and the call sits on a hot path, and the virtual interface when the choice arrives while the program runs. The advanced *Templates, Virtual Calls, `std::function`, and `std::variant`* lesson measures both, with the assembly each compiles to.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

class SizingRule {
public:
    virtual ~SizingRule() = default;
    virtual int size(double price) const = 0;
};

class FixedQty : public SizingRule {
public:
    explicit FixedQty(int qty) : qty_(qty) {}
    int size(double) const override { return qty_; }

private:
    int qty_;
};

class FixedCash : public SizingRule {
public:
    explicit FixedCash(double cash) : cash_(cash) {}
    int size(double price) const override { return static_cast<int>(cash_ / price); }

private:
    double cash_;
};

std::unique_ptr<SizingRule> makeRule(const std::string& name, double param) {
    if (name == "fixed") {
        return std::make_unique<FixedQty>(static_cast<int>(param));
    }
    if (name == "cash") {
        return std::make_unique<FixedCash>(param);
    }
    return nullptr;  // unknown: the caller decides what to do
}

// The compile-time form: no base class, no virtual.
struct CashRule {
    double cash;
    int size(double price) const { return static_cast<int>(cash / price); }
};

template <typename Rule>
int sizeOrder(const Rule& rule, double price) {
    return rule.size(price);
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, double>> config = {{"fixed", 300}, {"cash", 5000}, {"kelly", 0.5}};
    for (const auto& [name, param] : config) {
        const auto rule = makeRule(name, param);
        if (!rule) {
            std::cout << name << ": unknown rule\n";
            continue;
        }
        std::cout << name << ": " << rule->size(125.0) << " shares at 125\n";
    }
    std::cout << "template: " << sizeOrder(CashRule{5000}, 40.0) << " shares at 40\n";
    return 0;
}

