steps.body:
Every file that includes `engine.h` sees `Engine`'s private members, so adding one recompiles all of those files, and they all include whatever headers the members need. **Pimpl**, pointer to implementation, moves the private members into a struct that only `engine.cpp` defines:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

{{code}}

struct Engine::Impl {
    int position = 0;
};

Engine::Engine() : impl_(std::make_unique<Impl>()) {}
Engine::~Engine() = default;
void Engine::onFill(int qty) { impl_->position += qty; }
int Engine::position() const { return impl_->position; }

int main() {
    Engine engine;
    engine.onFill(100);
    std::cout << engine.position() << ' ' << sizeof(Engine) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
class Engine {
public:
    Engine();
    ~Engine();  // declared here, defined where Impl is complete
    void onFill(int qty);
    int position() const;

private:
    struct Impl;  // declared, not defined
    std::unique_ptr<Impl> impl_;
};
```

This Mac's `make`, on a project of three files that include `engine.h`, shows the gain. A new member in a plain class's header rebuilds every file, and the same member added to `Engine::Impl` rebuilds one:

```text
$ make    # a new private member in engine.h
c++ -std=c++17 -MMD -MP   -c -o main.o main.cpp
c++ -std=c++17 -MMD -MP   -c -o report.o report.cpp
c++ -std=c++17 -MMD -MP   -c -o engine.o engine.cpp
c++ -o app main.o report.o engine.o
$ make    # the same member in Engine::Impl instead
c++ -std=c++17 -MMD -MP   -c -o engine.o engine.cpp
c++ -o app main.o report.o engine.o
```

Define the destructor in `engine.cpp`, as `Engine::~Engine() = default;`. Without that, every file that destroys an `Engine` makes `std::unique_ptr` delete a type it has never seen, and clang stops with `invalid application of 'sizeof' to an incomplete type 'Engine::Impl'`. Pimpl costs a heap allocation per object and a pointer hop per call: fine for a big service object, wrong for a small value on a hot path.

A **singleton** is a class with one instance that any code can reach, usually as a function-local static, which is built on the first call and thread-safe since C++11:

```cpp
struct Logger {
    int lines = 0;
};
Logger& logger() {
    static Logger instance;  // built the first time logger() runs
    return instance;
}
++logger().lines;
std::cout << logger().lines << '\n';  // 1
```

That avoids the static initialization order problem from *A Process's Memory: Segments, Storage Duration, and Lifetime*, but it is still a global. Any function can change it, tests can't swap in a fake or reset it, and a function's signature no longer shows what it uses. Prefer one object made in `main` and passed by reference.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

// ---- engine.h: all that the files using Engine see
class Engine {
public:
    Engine();
    ~Engine();
    void onFill(int qty);
    int position() const;
    int fillCount() const;

private:
    struct Impl;
    std::unique_ptr<Impl> impl_;
};

// ---- engine.cpp: the only file that knows what Impl holds
struct Engine::Impl {
    int position = 0;
    std::vector<int> fills;
};

Engine::Engine() : impl_(std::make_unique<Impl>()) {}
Engine::~Engine() = default;

void Engine::onFill(int qty) {
    impl_->position += qty;
    impl_->fills.push_back(qty);
}

int Engine::position() const { return impl_->position; }
int Engine::fillCount() const { return static_cast<int>(impl_->fills.size()); }

// ---- main.cpp
int main() {
    Engine engine;
    engine.onFill(100);
    engine.onFill(-40);
    engine.onFill(25);
    std::cout << "position " << engine.position() << " after " << engine.fillCount() << " fills\n";
    std::cout << "sizeof(Engine) " << sizeof(Engine) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

